{0 OCaml OpenAPI}

[openapi-gen] generates OCaml clients from a supported subset of OpenAPI 3.x.
Generated clients use Fetch for HTTP, Jsont for JSON, and Ptime for timestamps.
The generator includes JSON/YAML spec parsing, schema codecs, operation functions,
and optional Dune regeneration rules.

{1 Generate a client}

{[
openapi-gen generate spec.json -o ./my_api -n my_api --regen
]}

The output contains [my_api.ml], [my_api.mli], [dune], and [dune.inc].
[--code-only] writes only the OCaml files. Regenerate with:

{[
opam exec --switch=5.2.0+ox -- dune build --profile release @gen
]}

Schemas are grouped into prefix modules. For example, [User] becomes
[My_api.User.T], and [UserResponseDto] becomes [My_api.User.ResponseDto].
Each schema exposes [t] and [jsont]; record schemas also expose [v] and accessors.
Operations with a component response normally live in that component's prefix
module. Other operations live in [My_api.Client]. The generated interface is the
source of truth for names and types.

{1 Configure Fetch}

The convenience constructor creates a curl stack:

{[
let client = My_api.create ~sw env ~base_url:"https://api.example.com/v1"
]}

Use [of_fetch] with an existing stack, including another backend or a mock:

{[
let base_url = "https://api.example.com/v1" in
let http =
  Fetch_curl.std ~sw env
  |> Fetch.with_credentials ~scope:[base_url]
       Fetch.Credential.[Bearer (fun () -> token)]
in
let client = My_api.of_fetch ~base_url ~max_response_bytes:(8 * 1024 * 1024) http
]}

Authentication, retries and rate/concurrency limits belong in the supplied
Fetch stack. Use [Fetch.with_credentials], [Fetch.with_retry] and
[Fetch.with_limits] as needed. The generator does not infer credentials from
[securitySchemes]. [create ?session] also accepts a supplied Fetch client.
[base_url] must be an absolute HTTP(S) URL without userinfo, query or fragment.

{1 Operation bodies and responses}

Path/query/header parameters currently take strings. Path values and query
names/values are percent-encoded; missing path parameters and dot segments fail.
Operation-level parameters override matching path-level definitions.

The selected request media type determines [body]:

{ul
{- JSON: a schema value encoded by [Fetch.encode (Fetch.Json.v codec)].}
{- URL-encoded form: a [(string * string) list] passed to [Fetch.Form.urlencoded].}
{- Multipart form: a [Fetch.Form.part list] passed to [Fetch.Form.multipart].
   Parts can include [Fetch.Form.stream] for file uploads.}
{- Other media: a [Fetch.body], with the selected Content-Type.}}

Optional request bodies use [?body]; required ones use [~body]. Declared bodies
are emitted for DELETE as well as POST/PUT/PATCH. Mutating methods and requests
with bodies do not follow redirects. Bodyless GET/HEAD retain Fetch's policy.

JSON responses use [Fetch.decode] with a declared Content-Type and bounded
nesting. The default response limit is 16 MiB, configurable per client.
Arrays retain their element/list types. Empty responses return [unit]; operations
with both value and empty responses return an option. Non-JSON responses return
bounded strings. All responses are closed before the operation returns or raises.
Streaming response callbacks are not generated yet.

{1 Errors and validation}

Non-2xx responses raise {!Openapi.Runtime.Api_error}. Error definitions are
selected by exact status, status range, then default. The error contains a raw
body and an optional typed/JSON/raw interpretation. Diagnostics are bounded to
64 KiB; larger bodies use a marker while preserving status. Error URLs omit query
values. Fetch decoding/transport errors remain [Eio.Io] exceptions with operation
context; cancellation and caller exceptions propagate.

{[
match My_api.User.get_user ~id:"123" client () with
| user -> use_user user
| exception Openapi.Runtime.Api_error error ->
    Format.eprintf "Request failed: HTTP %d@." error.status
]}

Schema guards run on encoding and decoding, including inline JSON bodies and
status-specific responses. They validate enums/const, bounds, decimal multiples,
string/array/object constraints and composition through an immutable reference
graph. Recursive values retain validation even when represented by [Jsont.json].
Primitive and array components expose scalar/list types and codecs.

Required nullable fields require presence, with [None] encoded as JSON null.
Optional nullable fields distinguish [None] (absent), [Some None] (null) and
[Some (Some value)] (a value). Their constructor argument is [?field:'a option];
[~field:None] explicitly emits null. Nullable defaults do not erase absence.

The schema model preserves OpenAPI 3.1 type arrays/boolean schemas and OpenAPI
3.0 boolean exclusive bounds. Guards apply dialect-specific nullability and
reference sibling rules. Numeric codecs reject fractional integers and non-finite
numbers; values that Jsont would encode as large-integer strings are rejected.
Invalid schemas and generated-name collisions fail before output is written.

{1 Current limits}

These codecs implement a subset of OpenAPI and JSON Schema. Request/response
readOnly/writeOnly directions, general format assertions, custom dialects and
vocabularies need further work. Known unsupported validating keywords fail
explicitly. Recursive and nullable object representations can be opaque JSON;
record codecs do not preserve unmodeled additional members.

Parameter serialization styles, cookie parameters, automatic multipart encoding
metadata, server/security overrides, full-width JSON integers, streaming response
APIs, and response header/status variants need further work. Multiple JSON success
shapes return status-validated generic JSON; mixed JSON/non-JSON shapes return
bytes. Output replacement is atomic for each file, not the entire directory.

The repository's [REVIEW.md] records the findings, applied fixes and regression
coverage. [OPENAPI_ROADMAP.md] describes the next implementation steps.

{1 Library modules}

{ul
{- {!module:Openapi.Spec}: specification types and codecs.}
{- {!module:Openapi.Codegen}: analysis and source generation.}
{- {!module:Openapi.Runtime}: URI, JSON, validation and shared Fetch helpers.}
{- {!module:Openapi.Schema}: dialect-aware schema guards and reference validation.}
{- {!module:Openapi.Nestjs}: NestJS error decoding and helpers.}}
